00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:07.170
It's your resident beauty nerd here:
Michelle of Lab Muffin Science and

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:12.000
today we're talking about anecdotal
evidence. It sounds sort of abstract but

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.299
it's really important to understand
because that sort of explains why my

00:00:15.299 --> 00:00:19.170
channel exists; why science is important
when it comes to figuring out what

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.130
actually works for beauty products and
for other things in life as well like

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.269
medical treatments and nutrition and
supplements. If you like videos about the

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:30.300
science behind beauty products, make sure
you click the thumbs up, subscribe to my

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:38.070
channel, and click the notification bell
so you don't miss any videos.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.910
Anecdotal evidence is when someone says that something worked for them or someone

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.539
they know. Basically it's a one-off
example of something working or not

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:50.070
working, for example, I use this cream the
next day a pimple disappeared and so

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.670
this cream must work for treating
pimples. Or... I took some multivitamins

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:58.649
then my cold got better, so that means
multivitamins fix my cold. Or my

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:00.989
grandma smoked continuously and died
at 92

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:04.199
so cigarettes aren't that bad for your
health. We see anecdotal evidence

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:08.549
everywhere in beauty: testimonials and
reviews are all forms of anecdotal

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:14.070
evidence and anecdotal evidence isn't
just limited to regular consumers- expert

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:18.330
opinions experience and case reports are
often forms of anecdotal evidence as

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.799
well. Medical professionals, scientists,
and beauty professionals all use and

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:27.689
misuse anecdotal evidence. It's simply
part of being human. All human beings

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:32.009
have evolved to naturally be subjective
and prone to biases, distortions in our

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:35.390
thinking that stopped us from being
purely objective and rational machines.

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:40.770
These biases actually helped us survive
through the ages but unfortunately they

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:46.460
don't help us as much in the modern
world.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:51.030
Anecdotal evidence can be very useful in
some situations. If you're going for a

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.390
beauty treatment you probably want an
experienced therapist rather than a

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.710
brand-new one, but the problem with
anecdotal evidence is while it sometimes

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:04.680
really useful it isn't reliable, and we
as humans have a bias to give it far

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.270
more weight than it deserves. Anecdotes
aren't good enough evidence for saying a

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.790
particular treatment led to a particular
outcome

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:15.960
and if anecdotal evidence disagrees with
good scientific studies and there's no

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.530
plausible explanation for how a
particular treatment might work, we

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.580
should go with what the science says.
Anecdotal evidence can be incredibly

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.780
convincing, it's a lot easier to believe
someone's heartfelt story than it is to

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.430
try to understand faceless, complicated,
and often scarily technical scientific

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:37.410
data, even if there are many someones
behind that data. This is part of the

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.950
reason why documentaries are so
compelling and why a lot of people who

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:45.180
are pushing pseudoscience choose to use
documentaries to present their case.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.400
And our love of personal stories was great
for human survival: if you avoided all

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.000
red berries because one person died from
eating them you end up living a lot

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.620
longer and passing on your genes, even if
not all red berries are dangerous.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.640
This quick mental shortcut allowed us to stayalive most of the time, but unfortunately

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.080
these quick mental shortcuts, these
cognitive biases that inform our gut

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:11.640
feelings, they also give us a distorted
view of reality. We live in a much more

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.500
complex world now and we need and have
much better tools to work out what's

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:21.030
going on then anecdotal evidence. I know
it can feel really unnatural to ignore

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:25.920
your gut feelings but a lot of the time
these gut feelings and instincts evolved

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.250
to help us survive under very [different]
circumstances than our modern world.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:33.000
Being aware of these sorts of biases
that we have towards anecdotal evidence

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.230
means we can compensate for it. This
makes us more aware of what actually

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:41.580
works and makes us much less vulnerable
to being manipulated by dodgy marketing.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.330
Being aware of these irrationalities
that we all naturally have means we can

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:49.410
make better decisions about what to do
and what to buy. So let's go through some

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.980
of the problems with anecdotal evidence.
I'll be focusing on the ones that are

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.599
more relevant to beauty products. Some of
these issues have fancy technical

00:03:56.599 --> 00:04:00.590
names, so I'll put the names of them on
the screen as I'm explaining them. One of

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:03.920
the biggest problems with anecdotal
evidence is that things get better on

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.879
their own a lot of the time. Back to the
pimple example, as most of us have

00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:11.749
probably experienced, pimples tend to get
better on their own in about a week, and

00:04:11.749 --> 00:04:15.379
if you're anything like me, you probably
won't bother doing anything about it

00:04:15.379 --> 00:04:19.459
until about three days in when it's at
its ripest. So there I am putting cream

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:23.479
on a single pimple, and it clears up
about three days later. To me, it looks

00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:27.410
like it works, but maybe in a different
timeline I use the light pen instead,

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:31.849
and the pimple cleared up, so it looks
like the light pen worked. Or in a third

00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:36.110
timeline I used a pimple patch and that
looked like it worked, but all along, if I

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:40.580
just left it alone it would have cleared
up as well, and so really none of them

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:44.509
worked. Or maybe say I use the pimple
cream and my pimple went away faster

00:04:44.509 --> 00:04:48.259
than normal and so it looked like it
worked, but that week I also used a new

00:04:48.259 --> 00:04:51.949
cleanser, and applied some exfoliant, and
went on a health kick, and ate a whole

00:04:51.949 --> 00:04:55.669
bunch of vegetables. Any of these could
have helped the pimple go away faster

00:04:55.669 --> 00:04:59.630
but I only linked the pimple cream to
the pimple in my mind because that's

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:03.380
what I expected to work. Even worse, a lot
of the time we forget about

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:08.060
longer-lasting treatments and we only
remember the last thing we used. I might

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:11.180
have even started on the contraceptive
pill which takes a while to start

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:14.810
working to reduce pimples, then I use the
pimple cream and then the hormones

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:18.380
kicked in and made the pimple go away
faster than normal, but I think it's just

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:22.639
the cream. These are all examples of how
one thing happening doesn't mean that it

00:05:22.639 --> 00:05:26.509
caused the next thing. Just because I
used the pimple cream and the pimple

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:30.710
went away, doesn't mean that the pimple
cream worked. Another issue is that

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:34.729
humans just aren't great at observing
things. I'm sure you've all had the

00:05:34.729 --> 00:05:38.330
experience where you and a friend can't
agree on something really mundane, like

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:41.509
what color shirt someone was wearing at
a party, or whether McDonald's

00:05:41.509 --> 00:05:44.599
cheeseburgers are actually getting
smaller. One researcher actually

00:05:44.599 --> 00:05:48.590
developed a method that could make 1/3
of people believe that a made-up event

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:52.430
happened in their childhood, and not only
did they believe it they would even tack

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:56.360
on extra details when they talked about
this event to someone else! We're really

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.889
good at filling gaps in our memory with
random stuff and tricking ourselves into

00:06:00.889 --> 00:06:04.849
believing things. With a pimple unless
you've been really carefully measuring

00:06:04.849 --> 00:06:08.330
it every day you can easily trick
yourself into thinking it's getting

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.470
bigger or smaller or it's going lighter or redder. If you

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:15.160
spent a lot of money on the pimple cream
and you really want it to work, you might

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:18.610
even subconsciously trick yourself into
believing that you can see a change,

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:22.720
forgetting all the times that you used
it and it didn't work. This is an example

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.620
of confirmation bias or wishful thinking,
and this effect can be bigger for more

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:30.460
expensive products, it's called post
purchase rationalization: where you don't

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:34.300
want to believe that you've wasted your
money on something that didn't work.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.870
As I've talked about in my e-book "The Lab
Muffin Guide to Basic Skincare" there are

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.530
a few ways that you can try to make your
observations as objective as possible, so

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:45.430
things like photos and skin diaries. But
while these can help you trick yourself

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:55.030
less, it still isn't foolproof. So ideally
how could we tell if a pimple cream

00:06:55.030 --> 00:07:00.760
really helps clear up pimples? What we
have to do is set up a fair test, so we

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:05.590
can specifically compare the pimple
cream with no cream at all. We'd make

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:09.370
sure that everything else stayed the
same. For example, if you had two

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:13.270
identical pimples on either side of your
face, then everything you ate should

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:17.440
affect both pimples equally. You could
also make sure that you used every other

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:21.700
skincare product equally on both sides
of your face or stopped every other

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:26.200
product altogether. This is called
"controlling the variables". If the pimple

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:29.890
cream worked, we'd also want to make sure it wasn't a one-off fluke, where the

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:33.790
lighting made the pimple look smaller,
or the pimple was a less severe pimple

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:37.660
than normal all the person scratched one
pimple in their sleep, but not the other.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:41.620
Instead of just trying the pimple cream
on one pimple, we could try the pimple

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.370
cream on lots and lots of pimples and
then get rid of the one-off fluke

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:49.870
results. If we wanted to test whether a
specific ingredient worked for pimples,

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:54.910
we could even make two creams: one with
the ingredient, and one without, and test

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:58.420
the two creams against each other to
make sure the people recording the

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:02.830
results aren't biased. We could label the
creams "A" and "B" and not tell them which

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:07.960
one was which. And that's pretty much
what science does. It sets up fair tests

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:19.100
that get rid of as much human
subjectivity and bias as possible. So hopefully that gives you a better idea of

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:24.250
why scientific evidence is better than anecdotal evidence. If science says one thing, but anecdotes say

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:28.270
another you should go with what the
science says. But one of the big issues

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:32.169
with skincare is that there's just not
that many high-quality scientific

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:36.400
studies around. The best ones are for
prescription products or for more

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:41.140
serious skin conditions like rosacea. A
lot of studies aren't fair tests, or they

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:44.590
test only one specific product that's
too different from a product you're

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:49.270
using to draw any convincing comparisons.
Or maybe they just didn't test enough

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:53.440
people to say much one way or another. So
if we don't have a lot of science to

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:57.070
guide us, that's where personal
experience and reviews become much more

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:01.570
valuable because it's the best we have.
But given how subjective and prone to

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:05.920
biases this anecdotal evidence is, there
are a few things we should keep in mind

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:13.930
so that we aren't misled. Reviews can be
a fantastic resource for finding

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:18.010
products that work well for you, but make
sure you don't believe every review you

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:21.970
read. As well as all of the problems with
anecdotal evidence that I mentioned

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:26.680
before, there are a few extra things to
keep in mind with reviews. Many companies

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:30.820
post fake reviews of their products. They
might pay someone to churn out tons of

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:35.050
positive reviews and post them online
under fake profiles. Even if they aren't

00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:40.600
fake, reviews can also be incentivized. If
a company gives out free samples, or pays

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:44.110
someone to review their products, this
conflict of interest can mean that the

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:48.220
review is more favorable than it might
otherwise be. But at the same time just

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:52.600
because someone was paid or it is a free
product, it doesn't mean that that's the

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:57.640
only reason why a review is positive. And
even if someone is reviewing a product

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:00.670
that they bought themselves, it doesn't
mean that it will be completely

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:04.510
objective. There are issues I mentioned
before like confirmation bias, where

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:08.020
someone sees a change because they're
expecting a change, and post-purchase

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:12.370
rationalization, especially if it's an
expensive product. There are also issues

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:16.510
if you're looking at multiple reviews.
you might see a lot of very good reviews

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:20.710
or very bad reviews, and that's because
humans are kind of lazy.

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:24.970
Unless something works really well or
really badly, we're not likely to go to

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.490
the trouble of writing a review. We can't
get a

00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:31.750
good idea of how likely it is we'll get
a good result, how representative these

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:36.459
reviews are. If we see ten rave reviews
we can't easily tell if it's a niche

00:10:36.459 --> 00:10:40.480
product where only ten people tried it
and they all loved it, or a mediocre but

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.230
popular product where thousands of
people tried it, but it only worked well

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:48.700
for ten people. Very few people update
their reviews, so we don't know if the

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:51.760
product continued to work well for
someone, or if they change their mind

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.020
after trying it for longer. Humans are
also better and remembering recent

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:59.500
things more clearly. Products with
immediate results will probably get more

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:03.459
positive reviews than a product that
works better, but takes a longer time to

00:11:03.459 --> 00:11:07.240
start working. If you've ever read
skincare reviews you'll know that

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.990
different people can have very different
experiences with the same product. In

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:15.220
scientific studies the people they tried
a product on are usually described very

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:18.940
specifically. You can work out how close
you are to the source of people they

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:23.260
tried the product on. But in reviews you
usually know very little about the

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:31.570
reviewer. So where does this leave us as
beauty consumers? This is where I talk

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:36.220
about what I personally think is a
sensible approach. You might disagree and

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:40.149
that is perfectly valid your best bet
for products that will work for you, are

00:11:40.149 --> 00:11:43.810
those that have enough scientific
evidence to back up its effectiveness,

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:48.459
but also have a large number of
reasonably objective positive reviews

00:11:48.459 --> 00:11:52.810
from people in a similar situation as
you. If there isn't any scientific

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:57.040
evidence for that exact product, then you
want there to be some evidence for its

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:00.760
active ingredients. If you don't have
that then you want a plausible mechanism

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:05.740
a scientifically sound explanation for
why it could possibly work and no

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:09.520
compelling scientific reason that it
wouldn't work. If anecdotal evidence

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:12.700
disagrees with the scientific evidence
then you should go with what the

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:16.990
scientific evidence says. The higher the
risk, both health-wise and in terms of

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:21.250
budget- the stronger that scientific
evidence should be. Anecdotal evidence is

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:25.300
still useful in helping us decide which
products to try, as long as we keep in

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:29.860
mind the limitations and don't treat it
as proof of something working. I hope you

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:33.190
enjoyed this video it's a little bit
different it's more about why we care

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:36.820
about science rather than the science
behind a particular product, so please

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:39.550
let me know what you think in the
comments. If you like videos on the

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:42.329
science behind beauty products, click
the thumbs up and you can subscribe to

00:12:42.329 --> 00:12:46.800
my channel. You can also find me on my
blog labmuffin.com and on Instagram

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:50.180
under Lab Muffin Beauty Science.

